// A function type carries its own calling convention.  A pointer to it is
// lifted as an ordinary C function pointer, so a convention that is not the
// target default is reported the same way as for a named function.  On 32-bit
// x86 a `__stdcall` callback pops its own arguments and a `__cdecl` one does
// not; only the first is reported.
//
// RUN: bash %strip-json-comments %s > %t.json
// RUN: %patchir-decomp -input %t.json -emit-cir -output %t > %t.log 2>&1
// RUN: %file-check -vv -check-prefix=WARN %s --input-file %t.log
// RUN: test -e %t.cir
// RUN: not %patchir-decomp -input %t.json -emit-cir -strict -output %t.strict > %t.strict.log 2>&1
// RUN: %file-check -vv -check-prefix=ERR %s --input-file %t.strict.log
// RUN: test ! -e %t.strict.cir
//
// WARN: Diag Warning: {{.*}}lossy lift [callconv-unsupported]: calling convention '__stdcall' of function type 'stdcall_callback' is not the default for i386; lifted with the target default convention
// WARN-NOT: Diag Error
// WARN-NOT: of function type 'cdecl_callback'
// WARN-NOT: of function 'callconv_function_type'
//
// ERR: Diag Error: {{.*}}lossy lift [callconv-unsupported]: calling convention '__stdcall' of function type 'stdcall_callback'
// ERR-NOT: of function type 'cdecl_callback'
// ERR: Skipping code generation due to prior diagnostics errors
